              "text": "{\n  \"query_text\": \"According to WHO guidelines, how is the child population defined for sodium intake recommendations, and what evidence-based reasons prevent the application of low-sodium salt substitute (LSSS) recommendations to this population?\",\n  \"target_population\": [\n    \"children (aged 2–15 years)\",\n    \"infants (aged 0–24 months)\"\n  ],\n  \"conditions\": [\n    \"sodium reduction\",\n    \"low-sodium salt substitute (LSSS) use\"\n  ],\n  \"draft_rationale\": \"To fully answer this query, information from both passages must be merged. The first passage (who-sodium-2012) defines 'children' for sodium recommendations as individuals aged 2–15 years inclusive, while noting the guidelines do not address infants aged 0–24 months. The second passage (who-lsss-2025) provides the reasons why LSSS recommendations do not apply to children: there is a paucity of data, no studies have examined discretionary LSSS use in children, and the only RCT on non-discretionary LSSS use (in bread) showed very low certainty evidence of little to no effect on blood pressure, meaning no conclusions can be drawn.\"\n}",
